\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs28\b NIWIS — System Report\par
\f1\fs22\b0 Date: 2026-09-28\par
\par
\b Executive Summary:\b0\par
NIWIS (National Integrated Water Information System) is a single-page application and backend service that integrates meteorological ("mateological"), satellite/remote-sensing, and hydrological datasets to produce drought monitoring, situational awareness, and decision support for South African administrative units. This report summarizes system architecture, data sources, core indicators, existing behavior, known issues, and recommended next steps for each of the three drought domains.
\par
\b1 System Overview\b0\par
- Purpose: Provide an integrated dashboard for monitoring and analyzing drought across meteorological, satellite/remote-sensing, and hydrological domains with interactive maps, time series, KPIs, and exportable reports.\par
- Architecture:\par
  - Frontend: React + Vite single-page application (UI components, Plotly charts, SVG sparklines). Key path: frontend/src/components/\par
  - Backend: Python services in backend/app/ exposing CSV-derived REST endpoints.\par
  - Data: CSV files stored in `csv's/` (e.g., hydrology.csv, mateological.csv, satelite.csv) and GeoJSON in frontend/public/.\par
\par
\b1 Meteorological Drought ("Mateological")\b0\par
- Data Sources: station rainfall time series (CSV), aggregated climate records, and derived forecasts generated by models (Prophet, SARIMA) present in `examples_trend_analyzer.py`, `Trend_Analysis.ipynb`.\par
- Core Indicators:\par
  - Rainfall anomalies (monthly/seasonal departures from historical climatology)\par
  - Percentile thresholds and standardized deficits (e.g., SPI-like calculations)\par
  - Rolling accumulations (3-, 6-, 12-month totals) and trend detection\par
  - Forecasted short-term rainfall (used for early warning)\par
- UI/Outputs: time-series plots, anomaly maps, alerts, and an analysis studio for forecasts and model comparison.\par
- Processing Notes: Preprocessing requires quality control of station data, gap-filling, and consistent climatology baselines. Notebooks and scripts exist for deriving trends and statistics.\par
\par
\b1 Satellite & Remote Sensing Drought\b0\par
- Data Sources: satellite-derived indices available via CSV exports and static geo assets in `frontend/public/csvs/` (e.g., NDVI/VCI/EVI time series).\par
- Core Indicators:\par
  - Vegetation health indices (NDVI, VCI, EVI) and anomalies relative to seasonal baselines\par
  - Spatial anomaly choropleths and time-lapse visualizations for early stress detection\par
- UI/Outputs: interactive choropleth maps, per-unit time series, and map overlays for thematic layers (vegetation stress, soil-moisture proxies).\par
- Processing Notes: Remote sensing pipelines must include cloud masking, compositing, and seasonal normalization. For scale, consider cloud-optimized GeoTIFFs or a tiling service.\par
\par
\b1 Hydrological Drought\b0\par
- Data Sources: `csv's/hydrology.csv` containing monthly metrics per district. Backend service `backend/app/hydrology_service.py` ingests the CSV and exposes endpoints: `GET /api/hydrology/stations?date=...` and `GET /api/hydrology/history?district=...&date=...`\par
- Core Indicators & Derived Metrics:\par
  - River discharge (instant and 12-month series)\par
  - Flow percentile metrics (e.g., P10), Streamflow Severity Index (SSI), Groundwater Index (SGI)\par
  - Reservoir storage, dam level percentage, dam inflow/outflow\par
  - Surface runoff and baseflow separation\par
  - District-level KPIs (7 key indicators), sparklines (6-month), FDC (flow duration curve)\par
- UI/Behavior: The `HydrologyDrought` React component fetches station lists and per-district history, and renders KPIs, sparklines, maps, variables tables, insights, and recommendations.\par
- Known Issues & Observations:\par
  - A `ReferenceError` related to JSX was fixed (missing curly braces around conditional option rendering).\par
  - Remaining behavior: graphs remain constant across different municipality selections despite backend returning district-specific JSON during backend tests. Likely causes: string mismatch for district identifiers (display name vs normalized key), missing `key` props on sparkline components preventing rerender, or backend returning a default payload when the district parameter does not match known keys.\par
  - Backend occasionally yields HTTP 500 on CSV parsing for some requests (needs defensive parsing).\par
\par
\b1 APIs & Notable Files\b0\par
- `backend/app/hydrology_service.py` — hydrology CSV ingestion and district history endpoints.\par
- `frontend/src/components/HydrologyDrought.jsx` — frontend UI consuming hydrology APIs and rendering charts.\par
- `csv's/hydrology.csv` — authoritative monthly hydrology table (river discharge, storage, runoff, baseflow, groundwater).\par
- `frontend/src/niwis/mateoData.js`, `Trend_Analysis.ipynb` — meteorological processing and model testing resources.\par
\par
\b1 Limitations, Risks, and Technical Debt\b0\par
- Large CSV files are present in repository history; recommend using Git LFS or moving raw archives to external storage to avoid repository bloat.\par
- Frontend-backend coupling on district names is brittle; introduce unique district IDs or standardized `norm_key` function for robust lookups.\par
- CSV parsing should be hardened to avoid returning raw 500s for malformed rows. Add structured error responses and monitoring.\par
\par
\b1 Recommendations / Next Steps\b0\par
- Immediate (user-facing):\par
  1. Normalize district identifiers between frontend and backend (prefer numeric or short stable IDs).\par
  2. Add `key={selectedDistrict+selectedDate}` to sparkline/plot components to force rerenders after selection change.\par
  3. Add temporary client-side debug logging to capture fetch URL and response for `history` endpoint (done).\par
- Short-term (stability & hygiene):\par
  1. Implement defensive CSV parsing; return structured JSON errors for invalid requests.\par
  2. Move large CSVs to Git LFS or external object storage.\par
  3. Add an error boundary in React to avoid full-app crashes on component exceptions.\par
- Medium-term (scale & performance):\par
  1. Convert hydrology CSV to Parquet or ingest into a lightweight database for fast queries by district/date.\par
  2. For remote sensing, adopt cloud-optimized GeoTIFFs and a tiling approach to reduce client bandwidth.\par
  3. Add automated integration tests for API responses per district to avoid regressions.\par
